#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52540afe-6bae-5db6-95a2-a9867a8f858b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417332} {:answer/id #uuid "52693f37-1a7d-5506-8ee4-6500aa2d7349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417333} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ec2c00-c765-5813-aaa4-789423b68551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417336} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c9d1db-461c-5c21-9d19-a6e9f1533a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417339} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5ef832-2edf-5758-ba7c-c1096ad3a8c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417340}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417332} :answer-field/id #uuid "f30ffaea-faee-50f8-bb72-c0bdccf87c9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "548bbae5-603d-5bc6-9f7d-4ca6dbc971eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-122670" :question/problem "![](images/64/64f02fa7dcd23b20e61801c0453d8fe7ed013936572e1ee63f748361edd4dec5.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $i$ and $j$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=i,\\,G(x,y,z)=-j\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $z$-direction, so the $k$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=k\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $k$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram doesn't correspond to any of the listed vector fields."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256697 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f1e18b0-1117-563b-8441-3490065e61e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d3/d3fc823cfcea400219e553f9e6911a312f0a14c7f39aafb49d1af3463cb3e46b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253939} {:answer/id #uuid "46e3f6a4-7353-56bc-8961-cc92fd971024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/0e/0ea540eb4a48d1fb1777e2d6397e6d38937e9795c4d810b48c0fda0801d1c9ba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254269} {:answer/id #uuid "64b24222-51a7-5272-9ca7-2db93a07e9b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ba/ba42a974a78df2c0d228e8f5ae543019841c8a7b47e38f02e139fe2d97b8ecf5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254572} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad5cb04-3d21-55c7-a532-d1bd2ca52b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f4/f4f0b2e96fa7ee0f1dca7d57a2815e93d77665c0285fa81f2a8b2c5965a39415.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254895} {:answer/id #uuid "bde88cfd-9cb5-56e6-ba40-080493eb0ad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/36/369ebf19405768bcca19f4981f0237647836250548b0a33f9b4fdba92d85ddc9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255365}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255365} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e297b46-c5d5-5311-943f-eb3962e376bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3f30aab0-000a-5430-bdac-144d62e4296d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122693" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =x\\,i+y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-1,1),$ $(2,2),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-1,1) & =-\\,i+j \\\\ ∇f(2,2) & =2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =2\\,i+(-1)j=2\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/36/369ebf19405768bcca19f4981f0237647836250548b0a33f9b4fdba92d85ddc9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f4df781-1911-525f-b679-c4fc003bb1e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/0f/0f45b194df67a060c195d4368050cdad9b8bfefab86b41d5eb63723631543d7b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253805} {:answer/id #uuid "4e60cd36-67ef-5e43-b4e0-2891d7e90e88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/92/92f46798ebeae9017c91ddae24e0f68cef81a1045314950bc4b56af003af0b42.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254344} {:answer/id #uuid "ca078818-e709-5a55-8db7-62d422c4af5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/6c/6c76d43daa86b2c8034e3f12592ee6c18bc533034cb255934ff7eb08b2d53623.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255466} {:answer/id #uuid "f4d5cca5-5d32-550a-85a6-767c2bc17710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/df/df6472974d246217341c69163c2336190980b5aacaa5b1a5c11cbb7126ddde86.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255815} {:answer/id #uuid "ff3b9331-f12b-5cc1-9824-f87b6a349f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/76/7618ae208ba53f12fb31988a3e0d0cb40f78ddc4348c5049bb09127039c4d965.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255916}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254344} :answer-field/id #uuid "7db82da5-3e98-5b0c-942d-8b5d0515f72a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e2b6964a-0bdc-5f5e-bdc8-f351c47c4755" :question/math-academy-id "q-122699" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=2y-x?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-x) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =-i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,2),$ $(2,2),$ and $(2,-2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,2) & =-i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,2) & =-i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-2) & =-i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/92/92f46798ebeae9017c91ddae24e0f68cef81a1045314950bc4b56af003af0b42.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256705 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39ead94b-aaa3-5df0-8bc4-db320602981c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1d/1da33de6ab53eee30cb7dbdba61a437fd9e3209437d061d2c546386981962942.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254178} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8a3268-82d7-5922-85eb-904111ce9e5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ab/abdb12ae2b6a67283ae65485e124b2a5264ab63a401e8993829f8b56704af479.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254512} {:answer/id #uuid "5fc84add-7dd1-5a0b-9987-6f006015797c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/5e/5e19f0874cbbfcf99c45951c98b19d941c39451c33887fbdcacdb75faeffdc57.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254527} {:answer/id #uuid "88fda897-7ef2-5696-9746-cc266403903e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1e/1e9b6c550e79abfd03d4ab0c0c7942b5185d24fd2e72203d1cb87c0895e3a6f4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254886} {:answer/id #uuid "c0c76fbf-13a6-5b8f-be2d-44312d2dbaa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/82/8270942dbaed9f5177e179104bb479802a39c48afa2a528b138b458c042a1556.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255387}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254178} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9de488d-3ab0-5329-8a1a-ee5aeb6ecbc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f32efae7-0f28-5e13-b999-daa26821b418" :question/math-academy-id "q-122713" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xy)?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-1,1),$ $(0,-2),$ and $(2,0).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-1,1) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}(1)^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)}{(-1)^{2}(1)^{2}+1}\\,j=i-j \\\\ ∇f(0,-2) & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-2)}{(0)^{2}(-2)^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2\\cdot 0}{(0)^{2}(-2)^{2}+1}\\,j=-4\\,i \\\\ ∇f(2,0) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0}{(2)^{2}(0)^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{(2)^{2}(0)^{2}+1}\\,j=4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1d/1da33de6ab53eee30cb7dbdba61a437fd9e3209437d061d2c546386981962942.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b07ebbb-ae2a-5805-a7b7-426af5bcb497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/13/135122e85da455699e45c1e276152b20fee0ac9d18441eb5620b1aa43654c81b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254314} {:answer/id #uuid "8fca9e4f-70ee-50c3-8e6c-d0cd30d777a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/25/2595c11a1b30fee9d76028010d2c7e608250c0d48ce0191d7da2a5412af6a110.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254930} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2f9076-3f65-544d-b6ed-94bd3f9e2b0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e4/e4f9011d4497ac01495c1dcde81d9ba19e9b48f9d78c36ca6259d3a2465983b7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255484} {:answer/id #uuid "dba6b19f-483e-5557-90ff-71eb95e4575c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/3b/3bf6311586ae40bb490414108bbf232f40e1e727da45bbdbe3123e60c17f6268.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255617} {:answer/id #uuid "e36ece5b-4544-54b9-8906-c7cdc5544ad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/71/716f99a985d8b4909e76f922fc3bf5b3350f9cb7496ae0a0a429421016a19267.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255684}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254930} :answer-field/id #uuid "fefeda5b-1048-597b-a49a-96c4ffaa9319" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7de89f2b-b3a4-52f5-bd83-f755a3097808" :question/math-academy-id "q-122738" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{(y-2x)^{2}}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{(y-2x)^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-2(y-2x) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{(y-2x)^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =y-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =-2(y-2x)\\,i+(y-2x)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(1,1),$ $(1,3),$ and $(-2,-3).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(1,1) & =-2\\cdot (1-2\\cdot 1)\\,i+(1-2\\cdot 1)\\,j=2\\,i-j \\\\ ∇f(1,3) & =-2\\cdot (3-2\\cdot 1)\\,i+(3-2\\cdot 1)\\,j=-2\\,i+j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-3) & =-2\\cdot (-3-2\\cdot (-2))\\,i+(-3-2\\cdot (-2))\\,j=-2\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/25/2595c11a1b30fee9d76028010d2c7e608250c0d48ce0191d7da2a5412af6a110.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256709 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d9715f6-880f-5e90-9ccb-eb9b334a39ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/fc/fc954607d17ae1eadaa0aba8d939343290a5c20f47bf61863cba9c42d9f558c9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253919} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6397c3-941a-58f5-887e-447432631c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/5e/5e9e75b66969e1acd7b1a90a5243a3a496168644a53d541851ba933a5f465553.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254186} {:answer/id #uuid "44afdd8c-abe1-588b-af9c-32a4909426e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/21/2100cd71f6bbbcc46d3f9f35853f65a9bf0d3f25bf3273db0d244c05221d758d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254251} {:answer/id #uuid "7fb78a7f-9fd3-5fa7-bde9-8571110c7992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/34/34e44b959282b0bc6c245643329e2b485d3209d89f304cf76c106718120a2f10.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254800} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a32930-3699-5cd6-84b0-329514a970d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/0c/0c69bb201b98cd3728527c562bf4e92cef3c48adae4e01725c2497a73c97a0af.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255252}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254186} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f725ff6-094a-5962-a13d-abc513c68fd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2b3ac067-5818-5125-bedf-bd1f05f196a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-122742" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\,i+xy\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-1,1),(1,2),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\,i+xy\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-1,1) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\,i+(-1)(1)\\,j=-3\\,i-j \\\\ F(1,2) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\,i+(1)(2)\\,j=3\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ F(2,-1) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\,i+(2)(-1)\\,j=-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/5e/5e9e75b66969e1acd7b1a90a5243a3a496168644a53d541851ba933a5f465553.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256710 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "423f3338-ffb1-59f0-b120-219b32a13003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/48/4837efc1a78060b2954fa58b26f35e7b2c6d810ce676959653f8e7eca0b5b476.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254234} {:answer/id #uuid "45a53f5e-4056-51ed-a116-9ca60d3c4976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e8/e87092c467b78bfe00651fb9752ed54c7f7784c83612fba2149063eba57952e9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254259} {:answer/id #uuid "71a298d1-6d06-55e3-ab94-8786b46031d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f2/f2835e43d43494d3319a6a0db0a411906f0424bdac8830425bad47723010d95c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254679} {:answer/id #uuid "74a2d6dd-b9fa-5a61-ab75-14dcdad78a4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/68/68d33b11d6476a0d35cb7486073e90e2755b46f1d4f935adc5dafb7087b15b99.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254702} {:answer/id #uuid "dc946e82-d393-53f9-bc76-247d76947cb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/58/58a106f2c0ac7aacd30f7ba8915fd2317a67efb73c1c48fe2d192a5d2576717a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255626}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255626} :answer-field/id #uuid "832d12ed-0f04-5945-adfb-d9b68890f3c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f0ef3069-54df-58ef-8ea9-a7adc99649e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-122744" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=-2x\\,i+{2}^{y}\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-4,2),(-1,1),$ and $(2,0).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=-2x\\,i+{2}^{y}\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-4,2) & =-2(-4)\\,i+{2}^{2}\\,j=8\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ F(-1,1) & =-2(-1)\\,i+{2}^{1}\\,j=2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ F(2,0) & =-2(2)\\,i+{2}^{0}\\,j=-4\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/58/58a106f2c0ac7aacd30f7ba8915fd2317a67efb73c1c48fe2d192a5d2576717a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256712 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "240a8926-bf1a-519e-9693-8f0bba9cbe1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b0/b014bb3efef5caa6a721173a7470f4600edc8207cae6adc3a77bffe217176e13.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253993} {:answer/id #uuid "48068bbf-dbb6-513d-a84e-da2cf1e1b829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/33/330bf1e0b9b2dff008a3e3878d5b2acba8e14addb637d637874492220cb08f2c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254278} {:answer/id #uuid "87cdf628-15c5-5fd3-b604-ae4e78fbbb87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/81/81ec06546a0ae47f254f42bfafabcf10c6aa7b776f49c59013c9c198bf1bf89a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254876} {:answer/id #uuid "c7647169-e091-5477-98ce-5a42c5fd0a32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e9/e959a73a075acd0e72e4cfa4edd09fa1710c450286ff574574208d42b2d897c4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255450} {:answer/id #uuid "fe49859d-ea70-5353-ac0d-504ccb94fae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d1/d150a7172d3a7e876cda685d9b096dd1f8d7d96a3349e355dc26562af75caa47.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255905}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254278} :answer-field/id #uuid "c344638a-8e0e-5a85-a726-eda285835155" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c5565e07-6be0-586e-b2a1-438c9383fcb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-122759" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=-\\frac{x}{y}\\,i-|x|\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-3,1),(2,-1),$ and $(4,2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=-\\frac{x}{y}\\,i-|x|\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-3,1) & =-\\left(\\frac{-3}{1}\\right)i-|-3|\\,j=3\\,i-3\\,j \\\\ F(2,-1) & =-\\left(\\frac{2}{-1}\\right)i-|2|\\,j=2\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ F(4,2) & =-\\left(\\frac{4}{2}\\right)i-|4|\\,j=-2\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/33/330bf1e0b9b2dff008a3e3878d5b2acba8e14addb637d637874492220cb08f2c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256718 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fb80c1c-95cb-5565-a077-e01dae2538e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/14/14c453b9fcf4670a2356b730253dd7f411b989abde303f059ebb5a9d559a4eff.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254362} {:answer/id #uuid "5575b773-fb45-568e-9e09-8e292ff7268e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e6/e6598c50e72417a0bff5514fa2b8ff9fba7c617cff10dc83e15472259d040846.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254415} {:answer/id #uuid "9d45adbb-a2ba-5573-a768-bab8773e9e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8e/8e30bba74fad98713f891950fd052d2ddd2cb2dc349bd9bcb7efe3333b7203dd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255052} {:answer/id #uuid "bb73aff5-0f60-5f0e-a385-48945789fb36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/41/417f9bd99ad7032ffac7ce258c5122d932b1bb1610237ef030f077ca56815bec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255341} {:answer/id #uuid "f66620e0-e12e-5bd1-b12e-36f3f0afecb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9c/9ce2dd3e0c639e4f520556c7f4955451eb599b35e0714aa326cb6efd74cfaa5c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255826}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255826} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d74b99d-539a-5a25-adea-708b48ca371b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ef1fc6b0-b283-5ecf-84c4-88b300df87f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-122796" :question/problem "![](images/20/201804549f48637076fcaad5252632527b937e01cc75c153bc1db26b246d2aa6.png)\n\nThe gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\nObserve that the given field vectors are perpendicular to the level curves in the following picture.\n\n![](images/f4/f4dc7eaf7ed7651ee38157c486f185ef3eecaf9ccb7e91df18e897c56c5c5e23.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/9c/9ce2dd3e0c639e4f520556c7f4955451eb599b35e0714aa326cb6efd74cfaa5c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00678095-4785-5a48-bfd2-04906406b23d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8b/8bd5b7575d1693935df0d25a259767f0dd267fc1153139cd55cebad75e3988fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253671} {:answer/id #uuid "03aaca50-6ba1-5767-8a1d-61289921be5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/4c/4c1a0035b37bc0137dc2ac164c980d538f9362feb3b439e663885f8aa7178124.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253699} {:answer/id #uuid "5936829d-c5a5-5317-93a8-8d046c07d07f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/fa/fa8bf282264f1647644cc15b6aece745718bd17617afad2c2530292bfed10b15.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254452} {:answer/id #uuid "70f6299c-8313-5e6f-a9c7-80abdfba5fd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/70/704a1e0712c0c2512e16ac03f1eeb7ecd55996c822eced54ac510f4719485c42.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254671} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf9db65-02b1-5896-a31b-6345273456e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f9/f9d6304bc9cc6b6e74235c66a7d70b96dbea2db44e9ed82606f6025075b76336.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255043}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253671} :answer-field/id #uuid "a46a2d28-3dee-5856-b9b2-7ac4fb2b706b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f67d0b79-608f-51fc-afe3-9623997cda39" :question/math-academy-id "q-122810" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=-1,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=-1,$ which gives the parabola $y={x}^{2}+1$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=0,$ that is, the parabola $y={x}^{2}$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=2,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=2,$ that is, the parabola $y={x}^{2}-2$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/77/77a54ef31c41e0246e3d337a69c7faa161194058e6a5c672ef24d86866a83faf.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move downward and away from the origin $O.$ \n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/4e/4e124d5e77ef9a28002aba9aac40f0b7eff1232dd1c08b6bca10b166469675c7.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n \n\n![](images/8b/8bd5b7575d1693935df0d25a259767f0dd267fc1153139cd55cebad75e3988fd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d16f010-8927-5a11-821b-dadc53e436d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/3d/3d5e0a4e1a1784167984a6dde093b97b14ebb0910ce56cd1d879782bfad6b7e8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253779} {:answer/id #uuid "118abcd0-7890-5af2-b48d-3e8745d6c268" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/3f/3fc34a2294e81abf04bfacf7d4367c0492a173af45f8f453a86b95a3a6028d4d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253822} {:answer/id #uuid "6523a047-fedb-5a31-a148-d33413134901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b6/b6b8dc1bcaf76374a580505308a9282b65e5da1b7d0961e2a6c30ba8ce6b2dd4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254577} {:answer/id #uuid "716fad33-4080-5fc3-bc31-939e1cf8b681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/cb/cb66a357917b9683a61b04aeab03ff60e359160f3c5e51dc4b282d6464666467.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254676} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff449c6-cc37-5b79-8ef9-32639dce72bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/5f/5fd011917170ef1f098b875060ad48d3ea5d33a1d16d369ae58614c53cd29c9c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254934}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254934} :answer-field/id #uuid "e493ba4b-c644-55d9-8213-f4543a4426f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d7e9bf88-89a8-589d-8711-7c4a07b86d32" :question/math-academy-id "q-122829" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=0,$ which gives the origin $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=1,$ we get $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=1,$ which gives an ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $1$ centered at $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=4,$ we get $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=4,$ which gives an ellipse with semiaxes $4$ and $2$ centered at $O$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/83/835b4206e04d064485d02635cfd4e2ad39a934b1d2b38296cc6463a265f5d931.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move away from the origin.\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/cd/cd8e0a10d162d30be895fa4ce49e3944e4e1a3ee9f79aa2fafe70c0edd2c0d61.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/5f/5fd011917170ef1f098b875060ad48d3ea5d33a1d16d369ae58614c53cd29c9c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256728 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "026f45f2-3480-501a-b813-16b2c86698bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/57/57f5858f1ccae96a169ee0aac3f106bd44b3de8282412389f4eb8d3ad8b66af9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253685} {:answer/id #uuid "27b268f0-4ffc-54a5-9920-488afedf2f20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c5/c5c0cc02a032916c8f9216673f23f3db81535828b14e51889942ffaf452ac3cb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254023} {:answer/id #uuid "67bd03df-6493-53ce-b107-795c206a4430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e7/e76b318c2f347bc59746ea0dc88c838ad46694724563867a0ee94318c826d887.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254608} {:answer/id #uuid "88069253-0215-50cc-b496-a51baed841a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/34/346e6bb41b8dd3b353dfedbc55eb25690396d5492d18199ba99a12523452db0a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254878} {:answer/id #uuid "9e64a607-79af-580d-9eca-019fa0b4a565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/08/08a6451e354eaaad23ef233a8c43d16512a2ed33257cd9e9b1257ef5eaaf558b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255065}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254878} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e428f18-510c-5a07-b305-8e470386def5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "43fea3a1-3b04-5702-a0ac-bdd3d7956101" :question/math-academy-id "q-122834" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=-1,$ we get ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=-1,$ which gives the vertical hyperbola ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=1$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0,$ which gives the pair of lines $y=\\pm x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=2,$ we get ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=2,$ which gives the horizontal hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/ce/cecc7f28c20523aac9f153022d64da4233ce995e05df3b3e2886b7fb3b20d089.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move from left to right in the right half-plane $x>0,$ and increases as we move from right-to-left in the left half-plane $x<0.$\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/03/037f46d3fdd190749c2aefe51222475fdd337a7b3ce53e6809048e3491a31a47.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/34/346e6bb41b8dd3b353dfedbc55eb25690396d5492d18199ba99a12523452db0a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259501 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "536e63b2-339e-55fb-9ef6-16302fa8d80b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257495} {:answer/id #uuid "562e0f5a-9f51-526c-b575-21a080e41feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257523} {:answer/id #uuid "67f3bbaf-7696-53c7-a073-517185ea4bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257700} {:answer/id #uuid "714228f8-6684-5ac7-831f-a8ce0050c5dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257778} {:answer/id #uuid "83d7a139-b408-5fd1-8726-70337664b912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257946}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257495} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f23d5c3-adfc-5e83-a5b1-c055960487eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b87e3ea6-f1f9-5cac-b915-0751187e2e15" :question/math-academy-id "q-123268" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-1,1)$ to $(1,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,1)$ to $(1,2).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+j$ and $b=i+2\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+j)+t[(i+2\\,j)-(-i+j)] \\\\  & =(-i+j)+t(2\\,i+j) \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(t+1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t-1$ and $y=t+1$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1+(2t-1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) \\\\  & =2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259503 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e37f599-d9fb-59d9-86fe-bf8eaa2a4cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12t-5)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256920} {:answer/id #uuid "1975ac33-0269-5ed1-88f8-8afee74b72b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "29{\\int }_{0}^{1}(10t-3)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257001} {:answer/id #uuid "2f82862b-d6d3-54c7-bc2b-fdf11833df09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{29}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+7)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257200} {:answer/id #uuid "33c69e8d-5ac9-5a07-ab6f-0a29d6e3e45b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{29}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12t-5)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257240} {:answer/id #uuid "fbcdea73-c430-5bfe-8af1-0bac80bdcafe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+7)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258984}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257240} :answer-field/id #uuid "05a11e2f-b331-55cb-99aa-ab3cf37cfaf5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "12a82c22-3ce2-5dee-95e7-fff601263913" :question/math-academy-id "q-123279" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=2x+y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-3,1)$ to $(2,3)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-3,1)$ to $(2,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-3\\,i+j$ and $b=2\\,i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-3\\,i+j)+t[(2\\,i+3\\,j)-(-3\\,i+j)] \\\\  & =(-3\\,i+j)+t(5\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =(5t-3)\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=5t-3$ and $y=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t-3)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t)j \\\\  & =5\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(5t-3)+(1+2t) \\\\  & =12t-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(12t-5)\\cdot \\sqrt{29}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12t-5)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259517 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f23753-d48c-590e-bac5-29003f108849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256826} {:answer/id #uuid "0ec75479-032b-576f-8363-7dd3f604bf87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256923} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc17017-3d39-5a47-a04e-8e25c1e82e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258184} {:answer/id #uuid "b6215184-663f-5e94-a809-e223798544a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258365} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0bd4a7-bd12-5381-bd48-060bce75559a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258422}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258422} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba54ee22-09fd-52a5-9f4c-6aad923451fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ed909600-8f3f-5028-beb2-725ed7978fc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-123363" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{2y}$ along the line segment from $(2,0)$ to $(2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(2,0)$ to $(2,2).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=2\\,i$ and $b=2\\,i+2\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =2\\,i+t[(2\\,i+2\\,j)-2\\,i] \\\\  & =2\\,i+2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2$ and $y=2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)j \\\\  & =2j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{2y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2+\\sqrt{2(2t)} \\\\  & =2+2\\sqrt{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+2\\sqrt{t})\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+\\sqrt{t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{[t+\\frac{2{t}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4(1+\\frac{2}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259518 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b297174-db28-5631-8ce7-0b6db400048f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257165} {:answer/id #uuid "527ae6e8-f296-5d07-9bf7-e7e1c033101a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4({e}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257488} {:answer/id #uuid "536119d2-aa2d-5141-9ba4-6effa0c108f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257493} {:answer/id #uuid "cef321a9-d7a9-52b7-b902-74641ba92360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{17}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258579} {:answer/id #uuid "f4e2f493-4761-5d47-968b-8b6a4da2f392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{17}({e}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258920}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258579} :answer-field/id #uuid "429e5a21-6fd3-5106-9c69-da9de1dbca48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cf9ff7ae-6e8a-5ec6-8ed3-2941337baefb" :question/math-academy-id "q-123366" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{3x+y}$ along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-1,4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0$ and $b=-i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0+t\\,(b-0) \\\\  & =t\\,(b) \\\\  & =t(-i+4\\,j) \\\\  & =-t\\,i+4t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-t$ and $y=4t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)j \\\\  & =-i+4\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{3x+y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{3(-t)+4t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\cdot \\sqrt{17}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}[{e}^{t}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259523 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d7255fb-f802-5c49-9cfe-5adaba813eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257598} {:answer/id #uuid "68a17d3d-a884-5f62-9df5-f64e05ce9233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2t-1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257710} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b81ee3-92f3-5eba-89d3-57cdd78eceac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{t}{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258623} {:answer/id #uuid "e1093dce-dfaa-5005-8422-51b5c1f47ce6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258741} {:answer/id #uuid "f4307381-173f-58dd-8909-ee98e9452ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2t+1}{t-1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258915}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258741} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f56fccf-0333-5fb0-960a-7e7e292ac899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bda2dabe-428d-5884-9572-9eaa49f96d4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-123431" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{y+z}{x},$ traversed along the line segment from $(-4,1,5)$ to $(-2,3,3)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-4,1,5)$ to $(-2,3,3).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-4i+j+5\\,k$ and $b=-2\\,i+3\\,j+3\\,k.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-4i+j+5\\,k)+t[(-2\\,i+3\\,j+3\\,k)-(-4i+j+5\\,k)] \\\\  & =(-4i+j+5\\,k)+t(2i+2\\,j-2\\,k) \\\\  & =(2t-4)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j+(5-2t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t-4,y=2t+1,$ and $z=5-2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-4)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+1)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5-2t)k \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j-2\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{y+z}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(2t+1)+(5-2t)}{2t-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2t-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{t-2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259524 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d242074-73a7-5026-be5b-fcc25f37d64b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}({e}^{3}+2)}{{e}^{8}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257185} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8b5c20-0f40-57fe-b657-0025210be1d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}({e}^{2}-1)}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257755} {:answer/id #uuid "c38917a5-66c0-5027-9c6a-4c2921700ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}({e}^{3}-1)}{{e}^{8}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258496} {:answer/id #uuid "d03cd34c-c515-5229-bf1b-a2f918c9c671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{{e}^{8}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258587} {:answer/id #uuid "e913cf48-832b-5dd0-a3e6-66ca570be19c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}+1}{{e}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258799}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258496} :answer-field/id #uuid "083a3adb-7461-5e3e-8a79-8a563a559eed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a763ea78-00d8-589b-9ab8-583f7360f0c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-123434" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x{e}^{yz}\\,\\text{d}s\n$$ along the line segment from $(2,-2,4)$ to $(4,-1,5).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(2,-2,4)$ to $(4,-1,5).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=2\\,i-2\\,j+4\\,k$ and $b=4\\,i-\\,j+5\\,k.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(2\\,i-2\\,j+4\\,k)+t[(4\\,i-\\,j+5\\,k)-(2\\,i-2\\,j+4\\,k)] \\\\  & =(2\\,i-2\\,j+4\\,k)+t(2\\,i+j+k) \\\\  & =(2t+2)\\,i+(t-2)\\,j+(t+4)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t+2,$ $y=t-2,$ and $z=t+4$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-2)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+4)k \\\\  & =2\\,i+j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{yz}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2t+2){e}^{(t-2)(t+4)} \\\\  & =(2t+2){e}^{{t}^{2}+2t-8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{e}^{yz}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+2){e}^{{t}^{2}+2t-8}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+2){e}^{{t}^{2}+2t-8}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{2}+2t-8$, $\\text{d}u=(2t+2)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+2){e}^{{t}^{2}+2t-8}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\sqrt{6}\\,{\\int }_{-8}^{-5}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\,{e}^{u}\\,{|}_{-8}^{-5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}({e}^{-5}-{e}^{-8}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}(\\frac{1}{{e}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{8}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}({e}^{3}-1)}{{e}^{8}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44832c8e-8988-5ee0-ac8b-67ae439d923f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257381} {:answer/id #uuid "70a0b147-68a3-5ec0-ae90-b9c82057aad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=-2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257773} {:answer/id #uuid "96d0c94c-faa8-5d39-b3c3-d13382b0eced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258114} {:answer/id #uuid "acdf99e3-cf16-5362-88ce-860873156446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=-i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258289} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d361e0-0169-5758-b933-86a6b5b677fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=-y\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258965}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257773} :answer-field/id #uuid "861c8be9-902d-5545-831a-501704ab8be1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1c63175a-a455-54b8-b784-f9c30e4507e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-123748" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bff60466ca8abba4fd849c046c02af3a4811279774975fc981cafad91d734ed5.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $j$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=-i+j\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-y\\,i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y,z)=-2\\,i.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b0b6380-f4e9-5ec2-bcc7-03b4ff06fa3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258003} {:answer/id #uuid "908d4a7b-6af1-5afd-acfb-b46fa54f9edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258053} {:answer/id #uuid "9abc8af7-a3e2-5316-8e7c-f13306f668fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258141} {:answer/id #uuid "9e8f5f06-c805-518c-a5e2-55a319c28965" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258177} {:answer/id #uuid "fd71ba70-4ea4-572f-abeb-acc8c1e00583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=i+{e}^{x}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258998}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258177} :answer-field/id #uuid "215c8d02-cce0-5acf-9590-b24a831a9a6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "589ce8fd-18ca-5044-bdd6-238376c16f16" :question/math-academy-id "q-123749" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f89653698d9c2bad0c2e6a77d4dad1953a9fd72f89cbfc08fd68aac802acd284.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $y$-axis. So, the $i$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=i+k,\\,K(x,y,z)=i+{e}^{x}\\,k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $y$-direction, so the $j$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=j\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $j$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}\\,j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram doesn't correspond to any of the listed vector fields."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0858fdee-1e5e-5d7f-ab1e-3a89c550e232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=-\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256850} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce989ee-22a0-54e3-b954-3393774f7d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=-{e}^{y}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257884} {:answer/id #uuid "accb76c2-2fde-50e7-8f9d-57f87a826ee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258288} {:answer/id #uuid "b7d26cd8-6acb-55c4-826a-fbc8652f0dd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258388} {:answer/id #uuid "ed5f02df-9d28-5ae8-8784-9a8977313908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=-2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258833}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258288} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3c30536-6d13-54e1-9db1-ae7eaa848eaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c9c83b42-2a92-533d-be7a-98b1b72e544d" :question/math-academy-id "q-123750" :question/problem "![](images/cf/cfc15b6202167211b7d6c0e8f6635dd9e571810d5296a209a4c317350604c4a4.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $j$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=i-j\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=-2\\,i,\\,H(x,y,z)=-\\,i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=-{e}^{y}\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram doesn't correspond to any of the listed vector fields."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259573 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fd295e1-1f3f-51b0-a531-01895286cbb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ae/ae77eaad51b8cd6bff4e62032946254da948e7643b45bd5e3a2343462ac457cb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257203} {:answer/id #uuid "7d496aba-750d-5723-bcf6-4b18fd5ca5ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/10/1067a6abc2249b31f3786a3f277a2db10d8f32cbacf971a550a0473243684da7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257888} {:answer/id #uuid "a6346f12-fe1a-52b3-ae64-8730d965aa8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/98/98a0e2e2cd960a8065d83ce53336a727709d0228d43e28d3aed216f755aaafaa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258231} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d4af53-2db6-53e6-a9dc-bf20307ab33f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/6c/6c5f1576f6882e6e72633e7eb707f77d2707e0afa76307403848a58a8f45689a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258349} {:answer/id #uuid "b9912de7-1c35-55de-a866-f90635b99433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/27/2725284d21ba0b2514e5043ce1f776338e0480e0bb50c26234fae0a7d12d404c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258406}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257888} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cb37ec1-d922-5b73-967a-f71d5b47a3a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "856afbeb-9f59-56e5-affb-452bd8050ce2" :question/math-academy-id "q-123882" :question/problem "Which of the following regions is *not* connected?" :question/worked-solution "A region $D$ is connected if every pair of points in $D$ can be joined by a smooth curve or a path in $D.$ \n\nAmong the given options, the only one that does not satisfy this definition is the following:\n\n![](images/10/1067a6abc2249b31f3786a3f277a2db10d8f32cbacf971a550a0473243684da7.png)\n\n \n\nNote that if we take two points $P$ and $Q$, as shown below, there is no path lying entirely within the region that connects them.\n\n![](images/2b/2bcaaf15d709d6396155bcdc9e8976bb037c9f341fc0b8863687958dbb0f40ff.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259574 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a04266f-fa97-5bc9-a110-7f2c8e8f9f80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ba/ba9b18a202db25c02b0907665d24fca75c9f4afc5babe898ff8f9c0227c7d447.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257297} {:answer/id #uuid "57b08ac5-e8a1-54f3-9c10-b1e40e64ae98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/82/82a33d59ae0060b0f1187415698ccf36ae45bd4ddc1fce0af74d01df74356ee1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257541} {:answer/id #uuid "88567626-0735-5cdd-944f-a824128be66a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/4a/4a67c845fe7993fe0c91784c92201db6ff5c15098db04dc588f5ebe0e8cdc1a1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257976} {:answer/id #uuid "89a42e11-a04d-52d9-9365-3edce26079d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/28/28105671ce0984f8a34da01d06dc8251244073ced9f775fbc3ede8d9f63bea21.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257991} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ce7c6c-439b-564f-a3e1-577034a5bf56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d6/d65f2e514477e8c935ddcf648f3073157489b083f1d108a81951e0499586f1c0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258790}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257976} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fb0f505-ea0d-5b4a-870d-b29b53c3b55d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "de4d0c85-6915-5695-9beb-a379a8504775" :question/math-academy-id "q-123885" :question/problem "Which of the following is *not* a simply connected region?" :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/46/46305c070e2563b4dacf8b31c88c46b860f003b20ffdff608a5485c09a91bd11.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/a0/a0459ddbabbd810e43db084f5dd174437340914b53ed227f6ef7ce2e3a0fc383.png)\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that does not satisfy this definition is the following: \n\n![](images/4a/4a67c845fe7993fe0c91784c92201db6ff5c15098db04dc588f5ebe0e8cdc1a1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b897e95-6751-561b-8fe2-d21f1c751aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462121} {:answer/id #uuid "02bf65b2-4dee-5c75-bd11-dc4da68376bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462122}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462122} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a76947c-5a6b-5e4a-a6f6-ac98081baf53" :answer-field/key "${R}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462814} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6739d37-a2f6-5db7-aff1-cdaafa0a656f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462123} {:answer/id #uuid "5c40bbe8-1385-5908-b8bb-8a4c13e1d9eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462124}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462123} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa4f729d-1314-52e4-8ead-056fdb497338" :answer-field/key "${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,1<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462815} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99d2c420-75a3-5cbb-b889-bcd9709d3efa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462125} {:answer/id #uuid "76cc7a7c-dfb6-5352-a448-d886f75dcc59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462126}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462125} :answer-field/id #uuid "44efefaa-2790-5c2f-9f2e-d8be27d699d2" :answer-field/key "${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "43a2cfcc-f62a-5511-8a9e-2111b0d76d4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-123887" :question/problem "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region ${R}^{2}$ is simply connected. It's a plane region without holes.\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,1<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4}$ is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region (a circle) with a hole. In this case, the hole contains all points on the disc ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}$ is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole at $(0,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${R}^{2}$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab02e9fa-a18a-5bfa-9a4a-7874d2595d15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462127} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3e1362-a334-597f-8485-f598c327597d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462128}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462128} :answer-field/id #uuid "21c918c9-a056-5a14-b108-cf36eee94f94" :answer-field/key "${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462817} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0820282f-a984-584d-bb09-4ba91b706ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462129} {:answer/id #uuid "022346a4-c6ef-5e3d-be52-9a15682c2b6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462130}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462129} :answer-field/id #uuid "40de5a38-0a97-54ce-bf6a-9b61bdad89f0" :answer-field/key "${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (2,1)}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462818} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb50d634-93dd-5dba-aa37-be77a33be6b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462131} {:answer/id #uuid "b52fb96c-4878-59cc-b25e-fe43a2f31ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462132}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462131} :answer-field/id #uuid "58585584-2f73-5c3c-ae4c-6fbf764850ac" :answer-field/key "${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>25}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "beac61f6-0d9e-58bf-85d2-405f0693eb4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-123916" :question/problem "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn. \n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}$ is simply connected. It's a plane region (a circle) without holes.\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (2,1)}$ is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole at $(2,1).$\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>25}$ is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole. In this case, the hole contains all points on the disc ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 25.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259579 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35574a80-0287-507b-88de-4c81ae7d6d13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462133} {:answer/id #uuid "c60a5879-3096-5a0a-919f-13e24b8584ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462134}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462133} :answer-field/id #uuid "24b3664c-da91-5036-9b62-f16a49bd6604" :answer-field/key "${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462820} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d36e33c9-d5fe-5406-bc31-4bd39f4e0587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462135} {:answer/id #uuid "afabceb2-3e43-58e3-a309-981fc0910b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462136}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462135} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e7c6623-9f65-56c6-8e08-bf71a010850f" :answer-field/key "${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462821} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d193f0e7-ab4d-5e93-ba8e-b2623717b696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462137} {:answer/id #uuid "6ee03dde-34a7-588a-a871-826d7b944ffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462138}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462138} :answer-field/id #uuid "182ce5ea-8de5-5895-8540-00589a1f0f62" :answer-field/key "${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ge 0}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462822} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b35bdd8-2917-505e-a829-1661198c4e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667910} {:answer/id #uuid "7e9de4db-21f4-5c66-a66a-eebc595afe00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667911}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186667910} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ee25fb5-de76-5a04-82e8-add7eff77e0e" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cd4cc5a1-c047-51e7-8736-2282f38e56c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-123923" :question/problem "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1}$ is *not* simply connected. The region contains all the points of ${R}^{2}$ except those on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ Thus, the region is formed by two disjoint pieces and, therefore, is not even a connected region.\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)}$ is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole at $(0,0).$\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ge 0}$ is simply connected. It's a plane region without holes.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ge 0}$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "302b8581-4a68-5c5c-8795-9744e9d4a2d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "76" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257206} {:answer/id #uuid "953e0310-1f6a-5dcb-9428-9966108f8f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258094} {:answer/id #uuid "b72e2f4d-4041-5680-a701-550b3147bc12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258378} {:answer/id #uuid "ca82cadd-75e5-598b-bacc-17ec432f7824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "109" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258541} {:answer/id #uuid "f5b95c82-3200-5da8-b666-432aa9c1aa8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "106" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258928}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258928} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b2dc29a-3749-5531-9860-c3324dac8a28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "003b8481-988f-56eb-a4c6-1362503d86f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-124002" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t},t+1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,4],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ r(4) & =⟨2,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(2,5).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(4))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(2,5)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =({2}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}+2+5)-({0}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}+0+1) \\\\  & =107-1 \\\\  & =106.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eea1d61-c31a-52a0-9c80-b705eeabdf54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256925} {:answer/id #uuid "32a24c80-640c-5f96-9d30-3886433c358a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257225} {:answer/id #uuid "89fe846b-c77b-5114-acc8-ae328a43149b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257994} {:answer/id #uuid "d080e35c-13c8-5910-bdbc-1433606170a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258595} {:answer/id #uuid "de2288dd-737a-5b6e-99cf-5d7174075a26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258714}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258595} :answer-field/id #uuid "13ff9e85-14c9-5103-9111-1d260075e876" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c23e8787-68ef-58ab-bf57-8f769ed64353" :question/math-academy-id "q-124003" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{e}^{2x}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,3{t}^{2}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,3)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(3{e}^{2\\cdot 1})-(0\\cdot {e}^{2(0)}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}-0 \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259586 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bb8367f-a159-5457-99f5-50133f64eb7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256893} {:answer/id #uuid "0fa182ef-4a0a-5db2-a08b-55a80052cb00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256930} {:answer/id #uuid "228efe5e-c3c4-564d-9dab-7a9a4edeb67d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257068} {:answer/id #uuid "3b05dbc7-a00e-579d-9183-316aebb35c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257306} {:answer/id #uuid "4e044364-8237-5de2-8e2f-2b3e611819bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257452}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257452} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e3f4676-5218-53c9-a375-46cf12254dde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "615b0135-e131-5b5f-9638-4296b1aa2a4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-124005" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,2t⟩$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$ and $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2\\pi )-f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ) \\\\  & =(2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\pi )-(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259587 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "543ba384-51b6-557c-bf16-43ac82ca0e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257502} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd16522-1981-538e-a666-611647c995ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258455} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c6cd51-6e8f-5103-b8e1-9cf9c4743823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+2e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258755} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc320e3-b118-5e6b-bdc4-5e336a48b133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258824} {:answer/id #uuid "f7bffceb-ddbe-55f4-ac78-cfd9d1162748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258955}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258755} :answer-field/id #uuid "63cb2a76-38cc-5ba0-833b-b7d3644b3675" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "26e83fe0-4239-5444-b048-589ba5747a08" :question/math-academy-id "q-124008" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x}+{e}^{y}+{e}^{z}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2},2{t}^{3}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,1,2)-f(0,0,0) \\\\  & =({e}^{1}+{e}^{1}+{e}^{2})-({e}^{0}+{e}^{0}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =(2e+{e}^{2})-3 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+2e-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
